WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:03.240
ED GLAESER: We live in an age of technological marvels and

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.985
these are constantly changing urban life and changing cities.

00:00:05.985 --> 00:00:08.790
20 years ago, no one had a single Facebook friend and

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:11.850
no one could repay lousy service with a bad Yelp review.

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:14.070
Now, urban revolutions are guided by Twitter.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:17.845
In this section we&amp;#39;re going to explore how technology is changing cities and

00:00:17.845 --> 00:00:19.610
how cities are changing technology.

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:23.570
For most of human history, technology moved at a pretty glacial pace.

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:27.230
One farmer hoed and reaped pretty much the same way that his grandfather did.

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:30.110
When technological change did occur, it tended to be in cities where

00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:33.870
incremental improvements in urban tasks like weaving would occasionally emerge.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:37.680
But then in the late 18th century, things started to change.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.680
First machines revolutionized cloth manufacturing.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.840
Then James Watts&amp;#39; separate condenser steam engines brought power to factories, and

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:46.120
to cities, and to transportation.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:48.770
And then the trickle of innovation became a torrent.

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:50.600
Cars, and radios, and air conditioners,

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.900
and television sets all radically changed the way humanity lived.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.940
They also enabled and changed cities.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:00.550
Try imagining the rise of Sunbelt metropolises like Atlanta without

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:01.530
the air conditioner.

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:06.110
The first question for this section is how do cities enable technological change?

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:09.960
If new ideas come from combining old ideas, does the diversity of

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.850
old ideas in cities make innovation easier, as Jane Jacobs once argued?

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:17.440
Does urban scale make it easier to find a market for new innovations?

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:20.020
Do new ideas spread more easily in cities, or

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.730
have these forces become irrelevant in the Internet age?

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:26.795
The second big question is how will future technological change impact cities?

00:01:26.795 --> 00:01:31.030
Self-driving cars, maybe Uber in the air, a more user-friendly version of Twitter.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.790
Some of these technologies may improve urban life.

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:37.220
Others may just exacerbate old city woes.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:40.290
Autonomous vehicles could easily lead to a lot more driving,

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:42.740
which might only make urban traffic worse.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:47.180
The final question concerns the interplay between technology and urban government.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:50.460
Some governments have tried to use technology to improve the delivery of city

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:51.570
services.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:55.580
Others have tried to regulate innovation and entrepreneurship more generally.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:58.500
How can cities balance the benefits and costs of change?

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:03.020
We can be sure that technology will continue to mold urban life, but

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:06.500
I&amp;#39;m less sure that we&amp;#39;ll know exactly how to get the most out of that technology.

